WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.591
A hospital bed is the most expensive place

00:00:03.591 --> 00:00:06.732
to receive care, so plans encourage alternatives.

00:00:06.732 --> 00:00:08.976
Preventive care catches problems early.

00:00:08.976 --> 00:00:13.913
Outpatient and ambulatory surgery centers handle procedures that once required admission.

00:00:13.913 --> 00:00:17.504
Home health care, skilled nursing facilities and hospice

00:00:17.504 --> 00:00:20.646
care let patients leave the hospital sooner.

00:00:20.646 --> 00:00:26.031
Urgent care centers and telehealth visits replace emergency rooms for minor problems.

00:00:26.031 --> 00:00:31.417
Plans steer members toward these settings with lower copayments and sometimes refuse

00:00:31.417 --> 00:00:36.803
to pay for inpatient care that could safely have been given elsewhere.

00:00:36.803 --> 00:00:41.740
Marcus’s outpatient knee surgery costs a fraction of an overnight stay.

00:00:41.740 --> 00:00:46.214
Utilization management checks whether care is necessary and appropriate.

00:00:46.214 --> 00:00:50.688
Prospective review happens before care: precertification or prior authorization

00:00:50.688 --> 00:00:54.665
for a planned admission, surgery or costly drug.

00:00:54.665 --> 00:00:58.642
Concurrent review happens during care: a nurse reviewer

00:00:58.642 --> 00:01:02.619
monitors a hospital stay and approves continued days.

00:01:02.619 --> 00:01:07.093
Retrospective review happens after care, examining claims for appropriateness.

00:01:07.093 --> 00:01:11.070
Second surgical opinion programs and case management for

00:01:11.070 --> 00:01:14.550
complex conditions belong to the same family.

00:01:14.550 --> 00:01:20.019
In Maryland, utilization review decisions that deny care are adverse decisions

00:01:20.019 --> 00:01:24.990
subject to the grievance and appeal process in Lesson 27.

00:01:24.990 --> 00:01:29.944
Marcus is covered under his employer’s plan and, as a dependent, under Elena’s.

00:01:29.944 --> 00:01:34.898
Without a rule, their son’s 4,000 dollar emergency visit could be paid twice.

00:01:34.898 --> 00:01:39.852
Coordination of benefits, a provision in group plans, decides which plan is primary

00:01:39.852 --> 00:01:44.425
and limits the total paid to 100 percent of the allowable expense.

00:01:44.425 --> 00:01:48.617
The primary plan pays as if it were the only coverage.

00:01:48.617 --> 00:01:51.666
The secondary plan pays the remaining allowable expense

00:01:51.666 --> 00:01:54.715
up to what it would have paid alone.

00:01:54.715 --> 00:02:00.050
The family never collects more than the bill, which honors the principle of indemnity.

00:02:00.050 --> 00:02:01.791
The rules are mechanical.

00:02:01.791 --> 00:02:06.578
A plan covering a person as an employee or subscriber is

00:02:06.578 --> 00:02:10.930
primary over a plan covering that person as a dependent.

00:02:10.930 --> 00:02:15.282
For a dependent child of married parents, the birthday rule

00:02:15.282 --> 00:02:19.634
applies: the plan of the parent whose birthday falls earlier

00:02:19.634 --> 00:02:23.550
in the calendar year is primary, regardless of age.

00:02:23.550 --> 00:02:29.208
If the parents share a birthday, the plan in force longer is primary.

00:02:29.208 --> 00:02:33.125
For divorced or separated parents, the custodial parent’s plan

00:02:33.125 --> 00:02:36.606
is primary unless a court decree assigns responsibility.

00:02:36.606 --> 00:02:40.958
A plan covering a person as an active employee is

00:02:40.958 --> 00:02:45.310
primary over a plan covering a retiree or laid-off employee.

00:02:45.310 --> 00:02:50.034
Marcus was born in September; Elena was born in March.

00:02:50.034 --> 00:02:54.757
Their son’s emergency visit produces 4,000 dollars of allowable charges.

00:02:54.757 --> 00:03:00.426
Elena’s plan is primary because her birthday is earlier in the year.

00:03:00.426 --> 00:03:05.622
It pays 80 percent after her 500 dollar deductible: 2,800 dollars.

00:03:05.622 --> 00:03:07.511
Marcus’s plan is secondary.

00:03:07.511 --> 00:03:12.235
It would have paid 3,200 dollars alone, so it pays

00:03:12.235 --> 00:03:16.486
the remaining 1,200 dollars, and the family owes nothing.

00:03:16.486 --> 00:03:23.099
If Marcus’s plan were an individual policy rather than a group plan, coordination of

00:03:23.099 --> 00:03:29.240
benefits would not apply; the insurance-with-other-companies provision from Lesson 17 would prorate instead.

00:03:29.240 --> 00:03:32.795
Cost containment and coordination attack waste from two directions.

00:03:32.795 --> 00:03:37.139
Containment reduces what the whole system spends by moving care to

00:03:37.139 --> 00:03:41.088
appropriate settings and reviewing its necessity before, during and after.

00:03:41.088 --> 00:03:45.038
Coordination prevents duplicate payment when two plans cover one person.

00:03:45.038 --> 00:03:49.777
Both rely on information: precertification requires the member or provider to call

00:03:49.777 --> 00:03:54.516
ahead; coordination requires the member to disclose other coverage on claim forms.

00:03:54.516 --> 00:04:00.046
A member who hides a spouse’s plan or skips precertification may face reduced benefits.

00:04:00.046 --> 00:04:05.180
Producers should explain both duties at enrollment so members use their plans correctly.
